    "q-106909": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106909",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${ℓ}^{2},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the squares of the terms are finite\n\nII. ${R}_{200}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $200$\n\nIII. ${\\text{M}}_{400}(R),$ the vector space of all $400\\times 400$ matrices over $R$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{2}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{2}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{200}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{400}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{200}[t])=200+1=201,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{400}(R))=400\\cdot 400=160\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
