      "dom_id": "step-q95387",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 800$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. ${R}_{500}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $500$\n\nIII. ${L}^{1}([-1,1]),$ the vector space of all functions  $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ such that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}|f(x)|\\,\\text{d}x<\\infty $",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R)$ and ${R}_{500}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R))=200\\cdot 800=160\\,000\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{500}[t])=500+1=501,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\n- ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of functions in ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,x,\\,{x}^{2},\\,…,{x}^{n}\\},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{x}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [-1,1]$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ of size $n,$ which imples that ${L}^{1}([-1,1])$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
